  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe aforementioned 500 mb ridging generally persists through the \nsecond half of the work week, keeping conditions dry and warm \nthrough at least Thursday. However, an approaching northern stream \ntrough late this week will help release the western CONUS cutoff low \nthat has been blocking our pattern thus far. This amplified \ntroughing will progress eastward into the Rockies and Great Plains \nregions through the second half of the week, shoving the ridge and \ncoincident surface high off eastward. The amplified flow pattern \nwill bring a better fetch of moisture into at least the western half \nof the state Friday into Saturday although the main surface low and \nbetter synoptic forcing generally stays north and west of Iowa. \nStill, some rain/showers may develop along the trailing cold front \nexpected to move into Iowa Saturday into Saturday night. These rain \nchances aren't overly high (around 20 to 30% per NBM) and heavily \nfavor northwestern Iowa given quite a bit of uncertainty with \nhow robust the boundary will be so far from the parent low. Even\nif the rain doesn't come to fruition, breezy conditions are \nlikely to develop Friday and Saturday with the increased \npressure gradients, again over northern and western Iowa. Cooler\ntemperatures begin to fill in Sunday into Monday, although the \nmagnitude of cooling will be dependent on how robust the cold \nfront is on Saturday.",
